SheepShaver on openSUSE
=======================

This package ships two programs:

SheepShaver
    PowerPC interpreter, no JIT.  Built with MEM_BULK (as the Windows
    port is), so the Mac address space is one block mapped anywhere in
    memory.  It works out of the box.  The "jit" preference is ignored.

SheepShaver-jit
    Upstream's default build: real addressing plus the PowerPC JIT
    compiler, which is much faster.  It must map the Mac low memory at
    address 0, which Linux forbids by default (vm.mmap_min_addr = 65536),
    and fails with "Cannot map Low Memory Globals: Operation not
    permitted".  To allow it:

      for the current boot only:
        sudo sysctl vm.mmap_min_addr=0

      permanently:
        sudo cp /usr/share/doc/packages/SheepShaver/90-sheepshaver.conf \
                /etc/sysctl.d/
        sudo sysctl --system

    With SELinux enforcing (the default on openSUSE Leap 16), the policy
    also denies mapping page zero until the mmap_low_allowed boolean is on:

      for the current boot only:
        sudo setsebool mmap_low_allowed 1
      permanently:
        sudo setsebool -P mmap_low_allowed 1

    This weakens a kernel hardening setting for the whole system: kernel
    NULL-pointer dereference bugs become easier to exploit.  On CPUs with
    SMEP/SMAP (Intel since Broadwell, AMD since Zen) most of that risk is
    mitigated.  Delete the file (and turn the boolean off) to undo.

Why not a JIT without the sysctl?  The JIT uses precompiled x86_64 code
snippets that assume Mac addresses equal host addresses; with MEM_BULK or
"direct" addressing it crashes at boot.

Both programs read the same preferences (~/.config/SheepShaver/prefs or
~/.sheepshaver_prefs) and accept a ROM file named "Mac OS ROM" (New
World) or a 4 MB Old World ROM image.
